        "title": "The Last House on Ferry Street",
        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from a short story.",
        "text": "The house at the end of Ferry Street had been for sale for eleven months, long enough that the realtor's sign had faded from red to a tired pink, long enough that the lawn had given up being a lawn and become, instead, a field. Neighbors had taken to parking their extra cars in its driveway, unofficially, the way people claim a space nobody's using.\n\nInside, dust had settled into the shape of furniture that was no longer there — a paler rectangle on the living room carpet where a couch had sat for thirty years, a ring on the kitchen counter where a fruit bowl had lived. The house remembered its former occupants more precisely than most of the neighbors did anymore.\n\nSomeone had left the porch light on, the last week the old man lived there, and no one had thought to turn it off since. It burned quietly through eleven months of empty afternoons, a small persistent signal to no one in particular that someone, once, had meant to come home."
