        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from a short story, narrated in the first person.",
        "text": "I told the insurance adjuster the fire had taken everything, which was true in the way that mattered to him — dollar amounts, replacement values, a checklist of rooms. I did not tell him about the drawer in the kitchen, the one that had stuck for years, that held exactly one photograph of my father laughing, an expression I otherwise couldn't quite remember correctly without it.\n\n\"Anything of particular sentimental value?\" he asked, pen ready, and I said no, not really, mostly just furniture, and watched him write that down as if it were a complete answer.\n\nWhat I did not say: that I had opened that drawer perhaps once a year, on no particular schedule, just to confirm the photograph was still there, still holding my father's face in that specific unguarded moment, and that somewhere in the space between the fire alarm and the front lawn, I had made a choice I was still making sense of — to save nothing, to grab nothing, to simply run — and that this, more than the house, was what I hadn't yet found a way to file a claim for."
