        "text": "Her mother had laid the garden path stone by stone the summer before she got sick, a project she never quite finished — it stopped abruptly two-thirds of the way to the fence, the last few feet still bare dirt where stones should have gone. For years afterward, whenever anyone offered to finish it, Rosa said no, not yet, though she could never explain, even to herself, what she was waiting for.\n\nShe walked it most evenings, out to where the stones stopped and back, the unfinished section a small, deliberate wilderness she stepped carefully around rather than through. Visitors sometimes asked if she planned to complete it. \"Eventually,\" she always said, which had stopped meaning anything specific years ago.\n\nThis evening, though, she brought a stone with her — just one, unremarkable, from the pile that had sat behind the shed since her mother died. She set it down at the exact place the path had stopped, not finishing anything, not really, but no longer leaving the gap entirely as it was, either."
