        "text": "The third stair still creaks — I never fixed it,\ngrew to like the warning, the small announcement\nbefore anyone reached the top. The kitchen window\nsticks in July; force it gently, upward first,\nthen out, the way you'd coax open something\nthat's decided, stubbornly, to hold its shape.\n\nThere's a loose board in the closet floor\nwhere I kept things I didn't want found\nby anyone looking casually — not hidden,\nexactly, just set a little apart from easy reach.\nI won't tell you what I kept there. That part\nisn't a fixture; it leaves with me.\n\nThe garden will come back in April\nwhether you tend it or not — it has learned,\nover the years I mostly ignored it,\nhow little it actually needs from anyone.\nI think I have learned something similar,\nliving here, though I couldn't tell you exactly\nwhat, only that I am leaving this place\nmore capable of taking care of itself\nthan I was when I arrived, and hoping\nthat counts for something, passed on\nlike the creak in the stair, unrepaired,\nbut at least now, fully understood."
