        "title": "From a novel, Chapter Eleven",
        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from Chapter Eleven of a novel. In this scene, Renata has returned to her childhood home to help her brother Tomas sort their late father's belongings.",
        "text": "Tomas had already started on the garage before she arrived, which didn't surprise her — he'd always been the one who couldn't sit still with grief, needed his hands doing something while the rest of him caught up. Renata found him surrounded by open boxes, holding their father's old fishing tackle box like it might explain something if he looked at it long enough.\n\n\"You're doing the thing,\" she said.\n\n\"What thing.\"\n\n\"The thing where you organize instead of feel anything. You did it after Mom too. Color-coded her closet the week of the funeral.\"\n\nHe set the tackle box down, carefully, the way he set everything down, like the world might notice if he was less than careful with it. \"Somebody has to sort through it,\" he said. \"Might as well be organized about it.\"\n\n\"I'm not criticizing.\" She sat down on an overturned crate. \"I just — I do the opposite. I've been sitting in my car outside for twenty minutes because I couldn't make myself come in.\"\n\nThis, he hadn't known. He looked at her properly for the first time since she'd arrived, taking in what she hadn't said in her voice — that this garage, this task, was somehow harder for her than it was for him, despite his own carefully maintained busyness suggesting otherwise.\n\n\"He would have hated this,\" Tomas said finally. \"All this — sorting, deciding what matters enough to keep. He never once, in his whole life, threw anything away on purpose.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Renata said. \"That's sort of the problem we're solving right now, isn't it.\""
