        "text": "(HARRIET's study. Law books line the walls, several visibly unopened in years. DEVON stands near the door, still in his coat, though HARRIET has not yet invited him to sit.)\n\nHARRIET: You look well. Successful. It suits you, apparently, working for people I spent a career trying to stop.\n\nDEVON: (a beat; he does not remove his coat) That's not entirely fair, Harriet.\n\nHARRIET: I never said it was fair. I said it was true. (She gestures, finally, toward a chair. He does not sit.) You could take off your coat. You're not being deposed.\n\nDEVON: I might be, a little. (He sits, though he keeps the coat on.) I need to know if you'll take the case.\n\nHARRIET: You need to know, or they need to know?\n\nDEVON: (looking at his hands) Is there a difference anymore?\n\n(A long pause. HARRIET moves to the window, her back to him.)\n\nHARRIET: There was a difference, once. I taught you the difference. (Still facing the window) I suppose I should ask what happened to it, except I think I already know, and I think you already know I know, and I think we're both going to sit here pretending this is a simple business conversation about a case.\n\nDEVON: Will you take it?\n\nHARRIET: (turning back to him, finally) Take off the coat, Devon. Then we'll talk."
