        "title": "From a novel, Chapter Six",
        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from Chapter Six of a novel, introducing two colleagues, Petra and Ilana, who have just been assigned to co-lead a project.",
        "text": "Petra had built her entire career on being early — early to meetings, early with deliverables, early enough with problems that they rarely became crises by the time anyone else noticed them. Ilana, by contrast, had built an equally successful career on being exactly on time and never a minute before, a precision she attributed, when asked, to \"not wanting to spend my whole life waiting around for things that haven't happened yet.\"\n\nTheir first planning meeting revealed the difference immediately. Petra arrived with a fully drafted timeline, contingencies mapped for the three most likely points of failure. Ilana arrived at the meeting's actual start time, listened to the entire plan, and then asked a single question: \"What happens if none of that goes wrong, and the actual problem is something you haven't thought to plan for at all?\"\n\nIt was, Petra had to admit, a fair question, and not one her careful advance planning had actually accounted for. She found herself, over the following weeks, watching how Ilana handled the genuinely unexpected — not with panic, and not with a pre-built contingency, but with a kind of calm, present-tense improvisation that Petra's own forward-looking anxiety had never quite allowed her to develop. Ilana, for her part, began arriving four minutes early to their meetings, though she never mentioned why, and never arrived early enough that anyone but Petra would have noticed the change."
