        "title": "From a novel, Chapter Eight",
        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from Chapter Eight of a novel. The main plot follows Theodora, a museum director, cautiously navigating whether to accept a much larger position in another city, away from her aging father. This excerpt follows a subplot involving Marcus, a junior curator on her staff.",
        "text": "Marcus had been offered the assistant curator position at a smaller gallery three states away — less prestigious, less money, considerably less job security than his current role. He accepted it on a Thursday afternoon, twenty minutes after the offer arrived, before he'd even discussed it with his partner.\n\n\"You didn't think about it at all?\" Theodora asked him, genuinely curious, when he gave his notice the following week.\n\n\"I thought about it for twenty minutes,\" Marcus said. \"That's still thinking about it.\"\n\n\"That's not really what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know what you meant.\" He smiled, not unkindly. \"You mean did I make a spreadsheet. Did I weigh the security against the opportunity, run the numbers on cost of living, wait for some external sign that this was definitely, provably the right call before committing to it.\"\n\n\"That's a reasonable way to make a major decision.\"\n\n\"It is,\" Marcus agreed. \"I've just never seen it work, is the thing. Not once. I've watched a lot of people wait for certainty before making the big decisions, and mostly what I've seen is people getting really good at waiting.\""
