        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from Chapter Fifteen of a novel. Gwendolyn, a talented surgeon, has built her reputation partly on never asking colleagues for help mid-procedure, regardless of complication.",
        "text": "Gwendolyn had not called for backup during a procedure in eleven years, a fact she mentioned, with evident pride, at least once during every resident's first rotation under her. \"You handle what's in front of you,\" she told them. \"Calling for help mid-procedure tells the whole room you've lost control of it. I have never lost control of a room in eleven years.\"\n\nWhen the complication arose during Mr. Alvarez's procedure — rare, but not unprecedented, exactly the kind of complication her own training had taught her required a second surgeon's hands to manage safely — Gwendolyn did not call for backup. She managed it herself, the way she had managed every complication for eleven years, and for forty extra minutes, alone, without the second set of hands the situation actually required, she very nearly managed it successfully.\n\nMr. Alvarez survived the procedure. He did not, as it turned out, survive it in the same condition he would have if Gwendolyn had made one different decision, forty minutes earlier, about what an eleven-year streak was actually worth measured against a single patient on a single table."
