        "intro": "The following is an original poem.",
        "text": "I have made my peace with the empty half of the closet,\nwith doing the crossword alone on Sunday mornings,\nwith learning to like the silence I once found unbearable,\nwith a version of contentment I didn't choose but built anyway —\n\nuntil the specific hour each evening the streetlights come on,\nwhen some old, unreasonable part of me still waits by the window,\nnot for anyone in particular anymore, just out of habit,\nthe way a phantom limb keeps insisting on an ache.\n\nI've stopped mentioning this part to anyone who asks how I'm doing.\nThe rest of the answer is true. I built a whole life around it being true.\nI just haven't found a way to explain the window, or the waiting,\nwithout it sounding like the peace was never really peace at all —\n\nwhich isn't quite right either. Some evenings, lately,\nI've started closing the blinds before the lights come on,\nnot to stop the waiting, exactly, just so I don't have to watch myself do it,\nwhich might be its own kind of progress, or might just be a better kind of hiding."
