        "title": "The Mill Town",
        "intro": "The following is an original poem combining several techniques.",
        "text": "They said the old mill gave the whole town its bread,\nits work, its Sunday shoes, its reason for the roads\nto run the direction they still, stubbornly, run —\n\nuntil the day the bank's letter arrived, brief and bright\nas a Christmas card, wishing the whole operation\nwell, sincerely, in its future endeavors elsewhere.\n\nThe town kept the roads. It had to; roads don't unbuild\nthemselves just because the reason for their direction\nis gone. Children still walk them to a school\n\nnamed after a mill owner none of them will ever meet,\npast a building whose windows have started, one by one,\nto go the specific gray of glass nobody's paying to keep clean anymore.\n\nSomeone put up a plaque last year. It uses the word 'legacy' twice.\nIt does not use the word 'closed.' It does not need to;\nthe whole town, every single road of it, says that part for free."
