        "type": "fiction",
        "title": "From a novel, Chapter Fourteen",
        "intro": "The following is an excerpt from Chapter Fourteen of a novel. Readers already know, from Chapter Nine, that Warren's business partner Marcus has been secretly diverting company funds for the past year, though Warren himself does not yet know this.",
        "text": "Warren whistled as he sorted the morning mail, setting aside the bills, humming through the catalogs, and finally reaching the cream-colored envelope with Marcus's familiar handwriting. \"Good news from Marcus, I'd bet,\" he said to no one in particular, smiling as he reached for his letter opener with the unhurried confidence of a man expecting nothing but good news.\n\nHe read the first line twice before setting it down to make coffee, unbothered, certain the rest would make more sense once he was properly caffeinated. It was, after all, just Marcus, checking in about the quarterly numbers, same as every month for the past eleven years.\n\nHe did not, that particular morning, notice that Marcus's handwriting looked slightly rushed toward the letter's end. He did not think to wonder why Marcus had called twice the previous evening and hung up both times before Warren reached the phone. He simply finished his coffee, finished the letter, and went about the rest of an entirely ordinary Tuesday, exactly the way a man does when he has no reason yet to suspect anything about his Tuesdays is about to change."
